# Copy this file to `.env` (gitignored — never commit real values) and fill
# in the values below before running `docker compose up`.
#
# Per TriTrack_Security_Model.md §4.6: secrets come from the environment
# only, every secret key here is present with an empty value (the required
# set must be discoverable without possessing a secret), there is no working
# default baked into docker-compose.yml for any of them, and the Postgres
# container itself refuses to start without POSTGRES_PASSWORD set — that's
# intentional, not a bug to work around.

POSTGRES_USER=
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_DB=

# Which host port the server is published on. Not a secret, so unlike the
# above it's fine to leave unset and let docker-compose.yml's default (8080)
# apply — set it here only if 8080 is already taken on your machine.
SERVER_PORT=

# WHO CAN REACH THE SERVER. Leave blank and it is published on 127.0.0.1 —
# this machine and nothing else. `curl localhost:8080/health` works; a phone
# on the same Wi-Fi does not.
#
#   SERVER_PUBLISH_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0   publish on every interface, so a phone on
#     the same Wi-Fi can reach it. It serves session tokens and observation
#     coordinates in PLAIN HTTP to everyone on that network.
#
# **Task 10.62 / finding J-2: setting this now makes the server REFUSE TO
# START until source identity is declared, and on Docker Desktop there is no
# declaration you can honestly make.** Docker's publish path rewrites every
# caller's source address to one gateway address before the server sees it, so
# §4.4's per-source rate limit becomes one bucket shared by every user and the
# audit log records a gateway instead of a person. The README used to walk
# through setting this line; it no longer does. Run
# scripts/verify_source_identity.sh --assisted to see what your host actually
# does (it needs two real off-host devices; the automatic mode can only screen
# — §4.4.4), and see TRITRACK_TRUSTED_PROXIES below for the answer the server
# is waiting for.
#
# Set it only on a machine whose own addresses are private — a laptop on Wi-Fi
# or a wired office network. On a host with a public address the server refuses
# to start in cleartext at all (Security Model §4.1), but a *container* cannot
# see its host's addresses, so on a VPS that refusal will not fire and this
# line is the only thing between a research dataset and the open internet.
# Configure TLS there instead.
#
# This defaulted to 0.0.0.0 until task 10.22; Security Gate C found that the
# artifact a clean checkout ran was cleartext on every interface (§7.3 B3).
SERVER_PUBLISH_ADDRESS=

# Transport security (task 10.14 / Security Model §4.1). The server binds
# LOOPBACK ONLY unless one of these says otherwise — a forgotten setting is
# unreachable rather than publishing session tokens to the network.
#
#   TRITRACK_ALLOW_CLEARTEXT_LAN=1  serve plain HTTP on all interfaces. This is
#     what lets a phone on the same Wi-Fi reach a laptop dev server. REFUSED at
#     startup when TRITRACK_ENV=production, and also refused when this machine
#     has a publicly routable address — §4.1 permits cleartext only to a
#     private-range target, and since task 10.22 that is enforced rather than
#     warned about. docker-compose.yml sets this itself (it describes the
#     container's own private bridge network); if you are running the server
#     with a bare `dart run`, set it here. For who can reach the *Docker*
#     server, see SERVER_PUBLISH_ADDRESS above — that is the one that matters.
#   TRITRACK_TLS_CERT_PATH / TRITRACK_TLS_KEY_PATH  serve TLS on all
#     interfaces. Set BOTH or neither — half-configured TLS refuses to start
#     rather than silently falling back to cleartext.
#   TRITRACK_ENV=production  turns on the production refusals above. Set it for
#     any real deployment.
TRITRACK_ENV=
TRITRACK_ALLOW_CLEARTEXT_LAN=
TRITRACK_TLS_CERT_PATH=
TRITRACK_TLS_KEY_PATH=

# SIGN-IN EMAIL (task 11.287 / Security Model §4.5, §4.6).
#
# TriTrack signs people in by emailing a one-time link, and this is the relay
# that carries it. REQUIRED when TRITRACK_ENV=production: the server refuses
# to start without TRITRACK_SMTP_HOST, because until task 11.287 a production
# server had no sender at all — every sign-in request answered 202 (as §4.5
# requires, whether or not delivery worked) and no link went anywhere. Nobody
# could sign in, and nothing said so.
#
# Any relay works: a campus relay, a commercial sending service, or a local
# catcher on a laptop. Nothing here names a provider.
#
#   TRITRACK_SMTP_HOST      the relay's hostname. Setting it is what turns the
#     real sender on, in any environment.
#   TRITRACK_SMTP_PORT      blank picks the port the TLS mode implies: 587 for
#     starttls, 465 for implicit, 25 for none.
#   TRITRACK_SMTP_TLS       starttls (default) | implicit | none.
#     `starttls` connects in cleartext and upgrades before saying anything;
#     a relay that does not offer STARTTLS gets nothing. `implicit` is TLS
#     from the first byte. `none` is REFUSED in production and refused with
#     credentials anywhere — a sign-in link is a live credential.
#   TRITRACK_SMTP_USERNAME / TRITRACK_SMTP_PASSWORD
#     both or neither. A campus relay that trusts this host's address needs
#     neither; a commercial sender issues a pair. The password is a secret:
#     a known placeholder is refused, and it is never printed.
#   TRITRACK_SMTP_FROM      the address links are sent from. The relay decides
#     whether this host may send as it — that is a conversation with whoever
#     runs the relay, not a setting.
#   TRITRACK_SMTP_CA_CERT   optional, the HOST path to a PEM file of extra
#     trusted root certificates, for a relay whose certificate an
#     institutional CA signed. The compose files mount it into the server;
#     leave it blank for a relay with a certificate from a public CA. There
#     is deliberately no setting that skips verification.
#
# The server checks the relay at every start — connects, negotiates TLS,
# authenticates, and hangs up without sending — and prints the outcome on a
# line beginning "Sign-in email:". A delivery that fails later is printed the
# same way, in the server's log, and NOWHERE ELSE: the person who asked for
# the link sees the same 202 either way. See server/README.md, "Sign-in
# email", including how to prove a link reaches a real inbox.
TRITRACK_SMTP_HOST=
TRITRACK_SMTP_PORT=
TRITRACK_SMTP_TLS=
TRITRACK_SMTP_USERNAME=
TRITRACK_SMTP_PASSWORD=
TRITRACK_SMTP_FROM=
TRITRACK_SMTP_CA_CERT=

# Source identity (task 10.52 / Security Model §4.4). Who does the server
# think each request came from? REQUIRED when TRITRACK_ENV=production — the
# server refuses to start without an answer, and that refusal is the control.
#
# The reason it is not optional: behind a reverse proxy, every request's TCP
# peer is the proxy. If the server does not know that, §4.4's per-source rate
# limit stops being per-source and becomes ONE BUCKET SHARED BY EVERY USER.
# Ten unauthenticated requests then deny magic-link sign-in to the entire
# deployment for 15 minutes, and every audit row records the proxy's address
# instead of the caller's. That is finding I-1, which survived four security
# gates because nothing ever asked this question out loud.
#
#   TRITRACK_TRUSTED_PROXIES=203.0.113.4,10.0.0.0/8
#     These peers are proxies you operate. X-Forwarded-For is read from these
#     addresses and from no others — the allowlist is of SOCKETS, never of
#     header values, so nobody can talk their way onto it. Your proxy must
#     OVERWRITE that header with the client address (proxy/Caddyfile does).
#   TRITRACK_TRUSTED_PROXIES=peer-verified
#     Nothing is in front of this server, the TCP peer IS the client, and you
#     have MEASURED that on this deployment with
#     scripts/verify_source_identity.sh --assisted, from two real off-host
#     devices. Reads no header, ever.
#
# `none` used to be the second answer and is no longer accepted for a server
# anything else can reach (task 10.62, finding J-2). It asserted a property of
# the network path that this project shipped as a default and never checked,
# and on Docker Desktop it is false: two distinct callers arrive as one
# address, so the per-source limit is one bucket for everybody. `peer-verified`
# says exactly the same thing about headers and additionally says somebody
# looked. Run the probe before writing it — if it reports COLLAPSED for your
# shape, that deployment has no per-source rate limit and must not be exposed.
#
# docker-compose.tls.yml sets this itself, to the proxy it pins; you do not
# need to fill it in for that path. Leave it empty for a loopback development
# stack, where one bucket and a per-source bucket are the same bucket.
#
# `peer-verified` is YOUR OWN measured claim about your own network, never a
# value this repository can generate for you. Full reasoning:
# CLAUDE_DOCS/SECURITY_SCOPE_AND_EXIT.md, "What TRITRACK_TRUSTED_PROXIES /
# TRITRACK_PROXY_SOURCE_IDENTITY mean".
TRITRACK_TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# How callers reach the proxy above (task 10.74, finding K-2). Required
# whenever TRITRACK_TRUSTED_PROXIES names one — including via
# docker-compose.tls.yml, which is the one variable that file does NOT fill in
# for you, because it is a fact about your host and not about this repository.
#
# An allowlist says which peer may speak for a client. It says nothing about
# whether the address that peer forwards is still the caller's. Your proxy
# forwards the address IT sees, and a host publish path in front of the proxy
# can rewrite every inbound connection to one gateway address before Caddy
# ever sees it — Docker Desktop always does. The allowlist then works
# perfectly and the per-source limit is still one bucket for everybody.
#
#   TRITRACK_PROXY_SOURCE_IDENTITY=probe-verified
#     You ran scripts/verify_source_identity.sh --assisted ON THIS HOST, from
#     two real off-host devices, and its `proxied` shape reported PRESERVED.
#     This is the only value that qualifies a deployment to be exposed. No
#     Docker Desktop host can earn it, and no host has been measured on this
#     project's behalf — measure yours. The probe without --assisted cannot
#     issue a PRESERVED at all: it holds only containers, and a container on
#     this host is not an off-host caller (§4.4.4).
#   TRITRACK_PROXY_SOURCE_IDENTITY=on-network-only
#     Nothing outside this host reaches the proxy either; every caller is on
#     the compose network, crossing no publish path, where identity survives
#     by construction. Not an answer for anything a phone can reach.
#
# Both values above are YOUR OWN measured claim about your own network, never
# a value this repository can generate for you. Full reasoning:
# CLAUDE_DOCS/SECURITY_SCOPE_AND_EXIT.md, "What TRITRACK_TRUSTED_PROXIES /
# TRITRACK_PROXY_SOURCE_IDENTITY mean".
TRITRACK_PROXY_SOURCE_IDENTITY=

# TLS-terminating reverse proxy (task 10.49 / Security Model §4.1, §8.6).
# Only read when docker-compose.tls.yml is layered on top of the base file —
# see server/README.md's "TLS termination" section for the full setup,
# including the one-time certificate this needs for the *origin* server (a
# second, separate pair from anything above).
#
#   TRITRACK_TLS_DOMAIN            the public site address Caddy serves — a
#     real domain for a real deployment (Caddy gets a real certificate from
#     Let's Encrypt automatically), or `localhost`/another address Caddy
#     treats as internal for a local self-signed verification run.
#   TRITRACK_TLS_ORIGIN_CERT /
#   TRITRACK_TLS_ORIGIN_KEY        the origin's own self-signed certificate
#     pair — generate once, outside the repository, never commit it.
#   TRITRACK_TLS_PUBLISH_ADDRESS   which host interface 80/443 are published
#     on. Defaults to every interface (0.0.0.0) — unlike SERVER_PUBLISH_ADDRESS
#     above, because bringing this override up at all is the explicit
#     decision to be reachable.
#   TRITRACK_TLS_PROXY_SUBNET /
#   TRITRACK_TLS_PROXY_ADDRESS     the compose network's subnet and the fixed
#     address the proxy takes on it (task 10.52). The address is what
#     TRITRACK_TRUSTED_PROXIES names, which is why it cannot be
#     Docker-assigned. Defaults are 172.31.242.0/24 and 172.31.242.10;
#     override BOTH, together, only if that range collides with something
#     else on this host.
TRITRACK_TLS_DOMAIN=
TRITRACK_TLS_ORIGIN_CERT=
TRITRACK_TLS_ORIGIN_KEY=
TRITRACK_TLS_PUBLISH_ADDRESS=
TRITRACK_TLS_PROXY_SUBNET=
TRITRACK_TLS_PROXY_ADDRESS=

# The read-only diagnostics console (task 10.13). Off unless
# TRITRACK_DIAGNOSTICS_ENABLED=true is set explicitly — leave all three
# blank for the normal, secure default. If you do enable it,
# TRITRACK_DIAGNOSTICS_TOKEN is the only thing standing between "reachable"
# and "readable" (there is no per-user login), so treat it as a secret;
# TRITRACK_DIAGNOSTICS_ALLOW_REMOTE defaults to false (localhost-only).
#
# Task 10.80: with ENABLED=true the server now REFUSES TO START rather than
# quietly serving nothing, if the token is missing, a known placeholder, or
# under 32 characters; if TRITRACK_ENV=production (Security Model §4.10); or
# if ALLOW_REMOTE=true without TLS configured (§4.1). Each refusal names the
# variable to change. Generate a token with `openssl rand -hex 32`; the full
# procedure, including the SSH tunnel that replaces ALLOW_REMOTE, is in
# server/README.md, "The diagnostics console".
TRITRACK_DIAGNOSTICS_ENABLED=
TRITRACK_DIAGNOSTICS_ALLOW_REMOTE=
TRITRACK_DIAGNOSTICS_TOKEN=

# Encrypted database backups (task 10.24 / Security Model §4.9, §4.9.1).
#
# TRITRACK_BACKUP_RECIPIENTS is a comma-separated list of age PUBLIC keys. It
# is not a secret — the whole point of public-key backups is that the machine
# taking them cannot read one — so unlike the Postgres credentials above there
# is nothing sensitive about the value that goes here.
#
# There is no default and no unencrypted fallback: scripts/backup_db.sh
# refuses to run when this is empty. **Generate the identity on YOUR machine,
# never on the server, and get the finished line for this variable, by running
# server/README.md's "Database backups" setup — it owns those two commands and
# this file used to carry a byte-identical second copy of them** (task 11.52;
# a copy that goes stale is wrong instructions somebody follows).
#
# List TWO recipients — yours and a second holder's — so that either can
# restore and neither has to send the other a private key. §4.9.1 is the
# custody arrangement and is worth reading before the first backup that holds
# real data: recipients are fixed when an archive is written and cannot be
# added afterwards, so this is a decision with no undo.
TRITRACK_BACKUP_RECIPIENTS=
# Where archives are written (host path). Copy this directory off the machine
# — an archive that only exists on the server is not a backup of the server.
TRITRACK_BACKUP_DIR=
# How many archives to keep. Default 30. Every retained archive is another
# full copy of the highest-ranked asset in the system (§4.9), so this is
# bounded on purpose.
TRITRACK_BACKUP_KEEP=

# ARCHIVE SIGNING (task 10.43 / Security Model §4.9.2, finding H-6).
#
# The recipients above decide who may READ an archive. These two decide who
# may WRITE one that a restore will trust, and until task 10.43 nothing did:
# the recipient string is public, so anyone who could create a file in the
# backup directory could write an archive that matched its checksum, declared
# a supported schema, decrypted under the operator's own key, and was then run
# as a Postgres superuser and renamed over the live database. `age` proves the
# bytes were not altered after encryption; it says nothing about who encrypted
# them.
#
# Generate the pair ON THE MACHINE THAT TAKES BACKUPS (unlike the age keys,
# which must never be there — this one has to sign unattended). The command is
# in server/README.md's "Database backups" setup, which owns it.
#
# TRITRACK_BACKUP_SIGNING_KEY is the host path to the .sec — the private half.
# Leave it blank and backups are still taken, still encrypted, and still
# restorable into a new database; they simply cannot be restored IN PLACE over
# an existing one, because their origin cannot be established.
TRITRACK_BACKUP_SIGNING_KEY=
# TRITRACK_BACKUP_VERIFY_KEY is the PUBLIC key from backup-verify.pub. That
# file has two lines and only one of them is the key, so let the command say
# which rather than a sentence here (task 11.37 — a value described in prose
# and retyped by a person is checkable by nothing). That command is in
# server/README.md's "Database backups" setup too.
#
# Not a secret, but it must be AUTHENTIC,
# so it belongs here, with the restore procedure, and never beside the
# archives: a directory that supplies its own verifying key verifies nothing.
# Accepts a comma-separated list so that rotating the signing key does not
# strand the archives written before the rotation.
#
# LEAVE THIS BLANK AND RETENTION NEVER RUNS — not just "restore in place is
# unavailable" above, a SEPARATE consequence (task 10.59, finding I-11):
# backup.sh can only prune an archive it can prove it wrote (§4.9.2), and with
# no key to check against it refuses to guess, forever, on every run, keeping
# every archive TRITRACK_BACKUP_KEEP would otherwise have deleted. Fail-safe
# and correct, but silent — a `backups/` directory with no size limit unless
# this is set.
TRITRACK_BACKUP_VERIFY_KEY=

# THE SCHEDULE, AND WHEN AN OLD BACKUP BECOMES A COMPLAINT (task 11.78, §4.9).
#
# Until task 11.78 nothing took a backup. server/README.md said to add a daily
# cron, which is a step a human performs and nothing checks, and the result was
# the harm task 11.70 had just fixed the other half of: an operator who believes
# they have encrypted backups whose newest archive is from setup week.
#
# `docker compose up` now starts a `backup-scheduler` service that takes one on
# this interval, and its healthcheck reports THE AGE OF THE NEWEST ARCHIVE — not
# whether the scheduler is running. `docker compose ps` is where you see it;
# `scripts/backup_status.sh` prints the same sentence.
#
# How often a backup is taken. A day is the unit of field work, so a day's
# bearings is the largest routine loss this design accepts.
TRITRACK_BACKUP_INTERVAL_HOURS=
# When the age of the newest archive becomes a complaint. Default 48 hours,
# CHOSEN rather than inherited: one missed daily run is a reboot or a slow dump,
# and paging somebody for that teaches them to ignore the one signal that would
# have told them backups had stopped. Two consecutive misses is a schedule that
# has stopped, and 48h bounds what is at risk to two days of observations — a
# bad week rather than a lost season. Lower it if a day of your data is worth
# more than a false alarm; raise it and you are choosing to find out later.
TRITRACK_BACKUP_MAX_AGE_HOURS=
# How often the scheduler wakes to ask. Default 3600 (hourly).
TRITRACK_BACKUP_CHECK_SECONDS=

# APPLYING THE RETENTION WINDOWS, AND WHEN AN OLD ROW BECOMES A COMPLAINT
# (task 11.79, §4.7).
#
# Until task 11.79 nothing applied §4.7's windows either — a year for sign-in
# events, 180 days for sync traffic, and nothing at all for decided join
# requests. So those windows described an intention rather than what was on
# disk. Worse, the command server/README.md gave could not run in the shipped
# container at all: it needed a Dart SDK the image does not have.
#
# `docker compose up` now starts an `audit-retention` service, and its
# healthcheck reports THE AGE OF THE OLDEST SURVIVING ROW — not whether the
# sweep is running. `docker compose ps` is where you see it;
# `scripts/audit_retention_status.sh` prints the same sentence.
#
# How far past its window a row may survive before that is a fault. Default 48
# hours, CHOSEN rather than inherited: the sweep runs on an interval, so between
# runs a row legitimately sits a few hours past its window, and a check with no
# grace would be red for part of every day — which teaches people to ignore the
# one signal that says retention has stopped. Two days past a 30-day window is a
# schedule that has stopped, not one that is late.
TRITRACK_AUDIT_RETENTION_GRACE_HOURS=
# How often the sweep wakes to ask. Default 3600 (hourly).
TRITRACK_AUDIT_RETENTION_CHECK_SECONDS=

# DESTROYING A PROJECT ON A SCHEDULE (task 11.290).
#
# An owner can schedule a project's destruction from the app. The server sets
# the date — this many days out, on its own clock — every member sees the
# countdown, the owner can cancel until it arrives, and on the date the
# `project-destruction` service deletes the project and every row in it. No
# device is involved in carrying it out.
#
# How many days a scheduled deletion waits. Blank means TRITRACK_BACKUP_KEEP
# (30), and that default is the honest one: the nightly backups keep that many
# archives, so a project cannot be "gone" faster than the backups let go of it.
# An institution with different rules sets its own number here.
TRITRACK_PROJECT_DELETION_GRACE_DAYS=
# How often the sweep wakes to ask. Default 3600 (hourly).
TRITRACK_PROJECT_DESTRUCTION_CHECK_SECONDS=
# How far past its date a scheduled deletion may survive before `docker compose
# ps` calls it a fault. Default 2 hours — one hourly cycle plus slack.
TRITRACK_PROJECT_DESTRUCTION_GRACE_HOURS=

# PURGING DELETED RECORDS AFTER THEIR 24-MONTH WINDOW (task 11.291).
#
# A deleted record survives on the server as a tombstone so a device that was
# away can be told what to delete. After 24 months — measured from when THIS
# SERVER accepted the delete, never from the phone's clock — the
# `tombstone-retention` service purges it, and a device that has not synced
# that project in 24 months is refused rather than allowed to bring the record
# back. On a server younger than 24 months the first sweep purges nothing and
# refuses nobody. Run scripts/tombstone_retention_status.sh before the first
# `docker compose up` on a server with history: it prints what the next sweep
# would purge and how many devices it would refuse, and starts only Postgres.
#
# How often the sweep wakes to ask. Default 3600 (hourly).
TRITRACK_TOMBSTONE_RETENTION_CHECK_SECONDS=
# How far past the window a purgeable record may survive before `docker
# compose ps` calls it a fault. Default 48 hours, for the audit sweep's reason.
TRITRACK_TOMBSTONE_RETENTION_GRACE_HOURS=

# WHICH USER THE BACKUP CONTAINERS RUN AS (tasks 11.70, 11.78).
#
# Normally leave these blank: `scripts/server_up.sh`, `scripts/backup_db.sh`,
# `scripts/restore_db.sh` and `scripts/backup_status.sh` all set them to
# whoever ran them, which is the right answer and needs no editing here.
#
# They matter because on Linux a bind mount passes host ownership straight
# through, and the backup containers write into your `backups/` directory and
# read your mode-600 signing key. A raw `docker compose up` cannot know who you
# are and gets the image's own uid 70, which owns neither — the scheduler then
# fails every cycle and reports itself unhealthy. That is loud rather than
# dangerous, but setting these two makes the raw compose line work as well as
# the scripts do.
TRITRACK_BACKUP_UID=
TRITRACK_BACKUP_GID=
